1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,096
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,096 --> 00:00:08,978
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:08,978 --> 00:00:13,700
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:18,619
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,619 --> 00:00:24,009
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:29,356
On the day, the book is up 158 dollars.

7
00:00:29,356 --> 00:00:33,863
Amneal Pharmaceuticals (AMRX) leads, +1.5 percent.

8
00:00:33,863 --> 00:00:36,297
Dropbox (DBX) lags at -0.7.

9
00:00:36,297 --> 00:00:41,165
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:41,165 --> 00:00:43,784
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

11
00:00:43,784 --> 00:00:47,435
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

12
00:00:47,435 --> 00:00:50,467
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

13
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:55,980
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

14
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:06,114
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

15
00:01:06,114 --> 00:01:14,949
6 positions, led by Arch Capital Group, ticker ACGL, Dropbox, ticker DBX, Cheesecake Factory, ticker CAKE.

16
00:01:14,949 --> 00:01:26,283
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

17
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:33,069
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

18
00:01:33,069 --> 00:01:35,623
Every morning OROS proposes.

19
00:01:35,623 --> 00:01:46,843
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

20
00:01:46,843 --> 00:01:51,040
Inside the book today: 5 positions up, 1 down.

21
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:53,594
Best, AMRX at +1.49 percent.

22
00:01:53,594 --> 00:01:55,418
Worst, DBX at -0.72.

23
00:01:55,418 --> 00:02:00,709
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

24
00:02:00,709 --> 00:02:08,372
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -2.73% per trade over 1428 graded positions.

25
00:02:08,372 --> 00:02:15,852
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

26
00:02:15,852 --> 00:02:21,326
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

27
00:02:21,326 --> 00:02:34,462
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 153 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

28
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:38,587
Over to the sports desk.

29
00:02:38,587 --> 00:02:48,198
Day 106 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

30
00:02:48,198 --> 00:02:54,638
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 108,743.

31
00:02:54,638 --> 00:03:02,134
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 10.9 thousand back at 97,820.

32
00:03:02,134 --> 00:03:07,805
Then the index fund, in gray, 11.6 thousand back at 97,185.

33
00:03:07,805 --> 00:03:10,496
The race has no finish line.

34
00:03:10,496 --> 00:03:14,917
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

35
00:03:14,917 --> 00:03:19,915
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

36
00:03:19,915 --> 00:03:21,838
Nothing is restated.

37
00:03:21,838 --> 00:03:30,708
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

38
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:39,840
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:42,077
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:42,077 --> 00:03:45,059
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
